# Literature Survey

## Unrolling Analysis of a SHA-256 Hardware Accelerator on Zynq-7000

**Author:** Amritha S (23BEC1368)
**Platform:** ZedBoard / XC7Z020 · Vivado 2022.2 · Vitis 2022.2
**Date:** July 2026

---

> **Verification notice.** I do not have web or database search access, so every citation below is reconstructed from memory. FIPS 180-4 and the well-known CHES / FPL papers are famous enough that titles and authors are very likely correct; years, venues and author orderings still need checking, and entries marked `[VERIFY]` are ones where confidence is lower. Confirm on IEEE Xplore, SpringerLink or the IACR ePrint archive before submission. Search strings are in §8.
>
> **Advantage of this topic:** the primary reference — NIST FIPS PUB 180-4 — is free, permanent and authoritative, and NIST's Cryptographic Algorithm Validation Program publishes additional test vectors. There is no ambiguity about ground truth.

---

## 1. Scope

| Cluster | Question |
|---|---|
| A | What is the algorithm, and what is the authoritative specification? |
| B | How do hardware implementations shorten the critical path? |
| C | What does unrolling / pipelining actually buy for a hash function? |
| D | What have FPGA implementations achieved, and on what devices? |
| E | What standards and tools govern the integration? |

---

## 2. Cluster A — Specification and Cryptographic Context

1. **NIST** — *Secure Hash Standard (SHS)*. **FIPS PUB 180-4**, August 2015 (originally 180-2, 2002).
   **The primary reference.** Complete specification of SHA-256: padding (§5.1.1), the initial hash values (§5.3.3), the K constants (§4.2.2), the six logical functions (§4.1.2) and the compression loop (§6.2.2). Contains worked examples.
   *Everything in this project's verification strategy derives from this document.* Cite it more than any other source.

2. **NIST** — *Cryptographic Algorithm Validation Program (CAVP), Secure Hashing test vectors.*
   Short-message, long-message and Monte Carlo test vector sets. **Use the long-message set for your soak testing**; it exercises multi-block chaining, which single-block vectors do not.

3. **NIST** — *Recommendation for Applications Using Approved Hash Algorithms*. **SP 800-107**.
   Security strengths and appropriate uses of each hash. Useful for one paragraph justifying why SHA-256 specifically, rather than SHA-1 (broken) or SHA-3 (a different construction entirely).

4. **Wang, X., Yin, Y. L. and Yu, H.** — "Finding Collisions in the Full SHA-1." *CRYPTO*, 2005.
   The theoretical break of SHA-1 that drove migration to SHA-2. Historical motivation for why SHA-256 is the deployed standard.

5. **Stevens, M., Bursztein, E., Karpman, P., Albertini, A. and Markov, Y.** — "The First Collision for Full SHA-1." *CRYPTO*, 2017. `[VERIFY]`
   The practical SHAttered collision. One sentence in the introduction establishes that SHA-256 is not merely conventional but necessary.

6. **Gilbert, H. and Handschuh, H.** — "Security Analysis of SHA-256 and Sisters." *Selected Areas in Cryptography (SAC)*, 2003. `[VERIFY]`
   Early cryptanalysis of the SHA-2 family. Cite for the claim that SHA-256 remains unbroken; do not overstate — this is a hardware project, not a cryptanalysis one.

---

## 3. Cluster B — Critical-Path Optimisation

**This is the cluster that matters most.** In SHA-256 the critical path is the five-input modulo-2³² addition forming T1. Every serious hardware paper is about shortening it.

7. **Chaves, R., Kuzmanov, G., Sousa, L. and Vassiliadis, S.** — "Improving SHA-2 Hardware Implementations." *CHES*, 2006.
   **The single most relevant paper to this project.** Introduces *operation rescheduling*: precompute part of T1 one cycle ahead so the adder chain is split across the register boundary, shortening the critical path without changing the round count. Also covers carry-save adder (CSA) use in the round.
   *This is the obvious "how would you improve it?" answer in your viva — cite it, and list it as documented future work.*

8. **Dadda, L., Macchetti, M. and Owen, J.** — "The Design of a High Speed ASIC Unit for the Hash Function SHA-256 (384, 512)." *DATE*, 2004. `[VERIFY]`
   Carry-save adder trees in the compression function. Relevant because your five-input adder is the bottleneck and CSA is the standard remedy.

9. **McEvoy, R. P., Crowe, F. M., Murphy, C. C. and Marnane, W. P.** — "Optimisation of the SHA-2 Family of Hash Functions on FPGAs." *IEEE Computer Society Annual Symposium on VLSI (ISVLSI)*, 2006.
   FPGA-specific optimisation: pipelining the message schedule separately from the compression, and balancing the two. **Directly relevant to your observation that the schedule unrolls for free while the compression does not.**

10. **Michail, H., Kakarountas, A. P., Milidonis, A. and Goutis, C. E.** — "A Top-Down Design Methodology for Ultrahigh-Performance Hashing Cores." *IEEE Transactions on Dependable and Secure Computing*, 2009. `[VERIFY]`
    Systematic treatment of unrolling and pipelining for hash cores.

11. **Sklavos, N. and Koufopavlou, O.** — "On the Hardware Implementations of the SHA-2 (256, 384, 512) Hash Functions." *ISCAS*, 2003. `[VERIFY]`
    Early comparative implementation study across the SHA-2 family.

---

## 4. Cluster C — Unrolling, Pipelining and Why Hashes Resist Both

This cluster supports the project's central argument. It is worth stating the argument explicitly rather than assuming the reader infers it.

12. **The structural argument** (make this yourself, in the report, and cite refs. 7 and 9 in support):

    > In a block cipher operating in ECB mode, blocks are independent. An unrolled implementation can therefore be pipelined: registers between stages hold the combinational depth per stage constant, maximum frequency is preserved, and throughput scales linearly with area.
    >
    > A hash function cannot do this within one message. Round *t+1* consumes the working variables produced by round *t*, and consecutive blocks chain through the hash value. Two unrolled rounds cannot be separated by a register without forfeiting the cycle-count saving that motivated unrolling. The adder chains stack in series, and maximum frequency must fall.
    >
    > The design is therefore a net throughput improvement if and only if the frequency retained exceeds the cycle-count ratio: **Fmax(B)/Fmax(A) > 34/66 = 0.515.**

    Deriving this inequality in the report — and stating it *before* the measurement — is the single most valuable paragraph you can write. Most student reports measure first and rationalise afterwards.

13. **Ting, K. K., Yuen, S. C. L., Lee, K.-H. and Leong, P. H. W.** — "An FPGA Based SHA-256 Processor." *Field-Programmable Logic and Applications (FPL)*, 2002.
    One of the earliest FPGA SHA-256 designs. Establishes the iterative baseline architecture your Configuration A follows.

14. **Sklavos, N. and Koufopavlou, O.** — "Implementation of the SHA-2 Hash Family Standard Using FPGAs." *The Journal of Supercomputing*, 2005. `[VERIFY]`
    FPGA implementations with area and throughput figures across the family.

15. **Multi-stream interleaving — the correct way to parallelise a hash.**
    Because *independent messages* have no dependency between them, N independent hashes can be interleaved through an N-stage pipelined round unit, giving N× throughput for roughly N× the working registers but the *same* round logic. This is the technique used in high-throughput hashing hardware, including Bitcoin mining ASICs.
    **This is your documented future work and the right answer if asked "how would you actually make this fast?"** Contrast it explicitly with within-message unrolling, which is what you measure.

