## 5. Cluster D — Reported FPGA Implementations

For your comparison table. Columns: device, architecture, slices/LUTs, Fmax, throughput, throughput-per-area.

| Work | Approach | What it establishes |
|---|---|---|
| Ting et al., FPL 2002 (ref. 13) | Iterative, 1 round/cycle | The baseline architecture |
| Sklavos & Koufopavlou, ISCAS 2003 (ref. 11) | Comparative SHA-2 family | Cross-variant area figures |
| Dadda et al., DATE 2004 (ref. 8) | CSA-based high-speed ASIC | The adder is the bottleneck |
| Chaves et al., CHES 2006 (ref. 7) | Operation rescheduling | Critical path can be shortened without more rounds |
| McEvoy et al., ISVLSI 2006 (ref. 9) | FPGA-specific pipelining | Schedule and compression balance differently |
| Michail et al., TDSC 2009 (ref. 10) | Unrolling and pipelining methodology | Systematic design-space treatment |

**Necessary caveat for the report:** these results date from 2002–2009 on Virtex-II, Virtex-4 and Virtex-5 class devices. Raw throughput comparison across fifteen-plus years of process technology is not meaningful. What transfers is the **structural relationship** — the ratio between iterative and unrolled configurations — and that is what this project re-measures on 28 nm silicon under identical constraints. Stating this limitation is more credible than a comparison table implying you have beaten a 2006 result.

