## 9. Recommended Reading Order

1. **NIST FIPS 180-4** (ref. 1) — read it properly, including the worked examples
2. **Chaves et al., CHES 2006** (ref. 7) — operation rescheduling; your future-work citation
3. **McEvoy et al., ISVLSI 2006** (ref. 9) — schedule vs compression, FPGA-specific
4. **Ting et al., FPL 2002** (ref. 13) — the iterative baseline
5. **Dadda et al., DATE 2004** (ref. 8) — why the adder dominates
6. **Michail et al., TDSC 2009** (ref. 10) — unrolling methodology
7. NIST CAVP vectors (ref. 2) — for the soak test
8. **Xilinx UG901** (ref. 21) — before deciding LUT vs BRAM for the K ROM
9. **Xilinx PG021** (ref. 19) — before touching the DMA
10. Stevens et al., CRYPTO 2017 (ref. 5) — one motivational sentence

---

## 10. Anticipated Questions and Answers

| Question | Answer |
|---|---|
| "Why no DSP slices?" | SHA-256 contains no multiplication. Only 32-bit addition, XOR, AND and fixed bit reorderings. There is nothing for a DSP to do. |
| "Why are the rotations free?" | ROTR and SHR are compile-time-constant bit reorderings. In fabric they are routing — zero LUTs, zero delay. Each Σ function is one 3-input XOR per bit. |
| "Why store only 16 schedule words?" | W[t] depends only on W[t−2], W[t−7], W[t−15], W[t−16]. A 16-deep window is sufficient; storing all 64 wastes 1536 bits for no benefit. |
| "Why does unrolling cost frequency here but not in AES?" | AES-ECB blocks are independent, so unrolled rounds can be pipelined and depth per stage stays constant. SHA-256 rounds are data-dependent — a register between them would forfeit the cycle saving, so the adder chains stack. |
| "Your speedup over software is only ~3×. Isn't that poor?" | It is expected. SHA-256 is 32-bit add, XOR and rotate — exactly what a general-purpose ALU does in one cycle each. Unlike AES's GF(2⁸) arithmetic, there is nothing the CPU must emulate. The value here is offload and deterministic latency, not raw speedup. |
| "How would you actually make this fast?" | Multi-stream interleaving. Independent messages have no dependency, so N of them pipeline perfectly through an N-stage round unit — N× throughput for N× registers but the same round logic. That is what high-throughput hashing hardware does. |
| "How would you shorten the critical path without unrolling?" | Operation rescheduling (Chaves et al., CHES 2006): precompute part of T1 one cycle ahead so the adder chain splits across the register boundary. Also carry-save adders in the five-input sum. |
| "Why is padding in software?" | Deliberate scope decision. Padding is byte-alignment bookkeeping and a length counter; compression is the expensive part. A hardware padder adds no insight into the unrolling question and is documented as future work. |

---

*End of survey.*
